fact WorkItem(id: "WI-787", description: some(value: "`spread_eta_args` reads `Value::Tuple.pos` ALONE, so a name-keyed tuple presents as ZERO components and an eta'd multi-parameter OPERATION cannot be applied to it -- the same defect WI-785 fixed in `match_tuple_pattern`, left unfixed in the sibling reader ~900 lines up the same file, and turned into a live REGRESSION by WI-786. eval/eval.rs ~1701 does `if let Value::Tuple { pos, .. } = &arg_values[0] { if pos.len() == arity { ...spread... } }`; it never consults `named`. WI-786 narrowed `classify_ctor_arg` so that user labels beginning with `_` stay NAMED instead of being hoisted into `pos` -- correct in itself -- but every component that moved out of `pos` vanished from this reader's view. CONFIRMED REGRESSION, driven A/B against a tree with ONLY `classify_ctor_arg` reverted (so WI-785 is held constant and the delta is WI-786's alone): with `operation sub2(p: Int64, q: Int64) -> Int64 = p - q` and `operation ap(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(<lit>)` driven as `ap(sub2)`, the literals `(_x: 3, _y: 10)`, `(_2: 3, _1: 10)` and `(_01: 3, _02: 10)` EACH returned `Ok(Int(-7))` before commit 93b23188 and now raise `ArityMismatch { op: \"function-value application\", expected: 2, got: 1 }`. Three programs that answered correctly now trap. NON-CONTRIVED PATH (this is the one that matters): a relation row is built all-named -- the row builder in eval/mod.rs constructs `Value::Tuple { pos: [], named: [...] }` because a row's field order IS the relation schema -- so `rule pr(?x, ?y) :- Rec(a: ?x, b: ?y)` then `pr.takeN(5).map(sub2)` raises ArityMismatch, while the byte-identical `pr.takeN(5).map(lambda (p, q) -> p - q)` returns `cons(-7, nil)`. Mapping a named OPERATION over relation rows is ordinary usage and is broken. SEPARATE PRE-EXISTING FACET (not a WI-786 regression, same root cause): `operation ap(f: (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64) = f((acc: 3, x: 10))` applied to a two-parameter op-ref traps identically both before and after 93b23188, while the same shape applied to `lambda (acc, x) -> ...` now works -- so the SAME source program has two verdicts depending only on whether the callee is an operation or a lambda. That asymmetry is precisely WI-785's motivating fold-callback shape with the callee kind swapped. ROOT CAUSE / ALTITUDE: `Value::Tuple { pos, named }` (eval/value.rs) exposes both halves as public fields with no owning accessor, so the \"`pos ++ named` is source order\" invariant WI-786 established lives only in doc comments and each consumer re-derives the walk. There are currently FOUR independent walks: `match_tuple_pattern` (eval/pattern.rs) and `detect_cycle` (eval/effects.rs) chain both halves correctly; `spread_eta_args` reads `pos` only (this ticket); `relation_project_run` / `relation_fix` (eval/builtins.rs ~1605/~1667) read `named` only -- and a driven case shows `rel.fix(_1: 3)` silently loses its column filter entirely. FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) minimal and local -- mirror the matcher at eval/eval.rs ~1701: `if pos.len() + named.len() == arity { pos.iter().chain(named.iter().map(|(_, v)| v)).cloned().collect() }`, which also closes the lambda/op-ref asymmetry as a side effect; (b) structural -- add `Value::Tuple::components() -> impl Iterator<Item = &Value>` and make the two halves private, turning the invariant from prose into a signature so that every remaining half-reader becomes a COMPILE ERROR rather than a runtime surprise; this is what would have caught the present bug, since WI-786 documented the invariant in three doc blocks across two files and the prose demonstrably did not reach a reader 900 lines away in the same file. (b) subsumes the `relation_fix` / `relation_project_run` gap and is the recommended direction if the extra scope is acceptable; (a) is the right immediate stop-the-bleeding change. Found by /code-review on 93b23188 (four of ten review angles independently built binaries and drove it). ACCEPTANCE: `ap(sub2)` over each of `(_x: 3, _y: 10)`, `(_2: 3, _1: 10)`, `(_01: 3, _02: 10)` evaluates to -7 again; `pr.takeN(5).map(sub2)` over an all-named relation row evaluates, agreeing with the `lambda (p, q)` spelling of the same call; a regression test drives the OPERATION and LAMBDA spellings of one call and asserts they AGREE end-to-end (using a fresh Interpreter per case -- a reused one returns a bogus Internal(\"deliver: parent frame had no awaiting state\") after any trap); if direction (b) is taken, `relation_fix` with a `_`-prefixed key no longer drops its filter; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T11:41:18Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-787", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, but three of this ticket's own premises were REFUTED by driving them at HEAD -- the ticket was filed against 93b23188 and WI-789/790/791/792/793/794/795 have landed since. (1) The ACCEPTANCE FIXTURES NO LONGER REACH EVAL. WI-791 made arity a child of the arrow, so a one-parameter slot whose parameter is a TUPLE is no longer satisfied by a genuinely two-parameter operation: 'operation ap(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64) = f(<lit>)' driven as 'ap(sub2)' now fails at LOAD with 'expected 2 arguments -- the parameter list this function value declares, got 1 argument', and the Function[A = (a,b), B = R] spelling fails with 'expected Function[...], got (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64'. The eval bug is therefore NOT reachable by the spellings the ticket names. It IS still reachable through a POLYMORPHIC hop, which is what the delivered tests use: 'operation apT[T](f: Function[A = T, B = Int64], t: T) -> Int64 = f(t)' driven as 'apT(sub2, (acc: 3, x: 10))' puts a type VARIABLE in the callback's parameter position, so the tuple's keying is invisible to the conformance check -- that program LOADS CLEAN and raises ArityMismatch{expected: 2, got: 1}, while the byte-identical 'apT(sub2, (3, 10))' returns Int(-7). Named-vs-positional is the only delta. (2) The ticket's 'pr.takeN(5).map(sub2) vs the lambda spelling returns cons(-7,nil)' A/B NO LONGER HOLDS EITHER: at HEAD mapElems REJECTS BOTH spellings at load ('expected a 1-parameter function ((x: ?x, y: ?y)) -> ?Dst, got a 2-parameter function'), the lambda included. The relation-row acceptance was met by routing the row through the same polymorphic hop; the delivered test asserts the OPERATION and LAMBDA spellings AGREE end-to-end (both cons(-7,nil)) rather than pinning either to a literal. (3) 'a driven case shows rel.fix(_1: 3) silently loses its column filter entirely' DID NOT REPRODUCE: the typer's Without reduction rejects it at LOAD -- 'Without drops the field _1, which is not a column of the base schema (a captured argument names no column to restrict)'. It is loud, not silent. The runtime half-read was still closed, but as a guard for a programmatically-built spec, not a live bug. DIRECTION TAKEN: (b) scaled down. Added Value::tuple_components() -> Option<TupleComponents> (eval/value.rs), an O(1)-len handle owning the 'pos ++ named is source order' invariant; migrated spread_eta_args (THE fix), match_tuple_pattern, and detect_cycle onto it; relation_project_run/relation_fix now share spec_record_fields, which refuses a POSITIONAL component loudly instead of reading the empty record as an identity filter. (b) AS LITERALLY SPECIFIED IS REFUTED, not merely expensive: making the halves private cannot 'turn every remaining half-reader into a COMPILE ERROR' because LEGITIMATE half-readers exist -- TermView for Value (kb/term_view.rs) reads pos by INDEX in pos_arg and named by SYMBOL in named_arg/named_keys, which IS the trait's contract (it mirrors Term::Fn{pos_args, named_args}), and field_access (eval/builtins.rs) reads both halves through DIFFERENT access paths (named by short name, pos by positional_label_index). Privatizing would false-positive on four sanctioned readers; it is deliverable only with an escape hatch, a materially different proposal. That argument is now recorded IN the tuple_components doc so it is not re-litigated. Also recorded there: this does NOT generalize to Value::Entity despite the identical layout, because finish_constructor canonicalizes an entity's named into DECLARED field order while a tuple is exempted from that canonicalization precisely so SOURCE order survives -- one shared accessor would be a single iterator silently meaning two different things. TESTS: 6 in wi787_eta_spread_named_tuple_test.rs. Verified NOT BLIND by reverting only spread_eta_args: the 3 regression tests fail with exactly ArityMismatch{expected:2,got:1} while the 3 controls (positional spread, arity strictness, parser mixing-refusal) stay green. One further measured fact worth recording: a tuple literal MIXING positional and named components is refused by the PARSER, so the prefix-split shape is not constructible today -- pinned by a test rather than claimed in prose. Full workspace green: 3251 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-21T11:41:11Z")
